 "history": [
  {
   "note": "Pre-redesign snapshot, kept for the record. Every one of these controls was verified present on every iteration of the redesign; the two renames and four removals below are the only differences, and each was reviewed. Re-captured after the ninth page so the gate also guards the controls the redesign ADDED — it was protecting 72 of 101, which is a narrower promise than its name makes.",
   "captured_totals": {
    "http://127.0.0.1:8097/": 72,
    "http://192.168.50.1:8088/": 88,
    "http://192.168.2.1:8088/": 88
   }
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-08-15",
   "source": "all tracked targets",
   "control": "⛓ Subscription",
   "decision": "removed",
   "why": "The client-export subscription action was intentionally retired. Connection import remains available through Add connection, but the panel no longer publishes a client subscription URL. The historical entry stays in each target capture and this global declaration makes the reviewed removal explicit without weakening checks for the remaining Connections controls."
  },
  {
   "date": "2026-07-29",
   "source": "http://192.168.50.1:8088/",
   "control": "Remove <object>",
   "decision": "removed",
   "why": "This router's only installed engine (sing-box) is owned by opkg, and the panel refuses to remove a package-manager-owned binary — UninstallPrecheck returns a 409 before anything is stopped. Offering the button could only ever produce that refusal. It flickered in and out before the ownership-unknown fix because a locked opkg was being read as 'not owned'; it is now consistently absent here and still present on any router where a removal would succeed."
  }
 ]
